### Strongest proof attack

Attack the (D=108\to(18,12;\gamma^3)) pair by **ramification
absorption**, then lift the resulting obstruction into the tree coefficient
complex.  Monomial-Jacobian pairs without inherited data are plentiful: for
integers (d\ge r\ge1) and (a\ne0),

\[
 Q_0=\pi^d+a\gamma^r,\qquad
 P_0=\pi^{d+1}+{d+1\over d}a\gamma^r\pi
\]

satisfy, by direct differentiation,

\[
 J(P_0,Q_0)=-{r(d+1)\over d}a^2\gamma^{2r-1}.
\]

For (d=r=2), this is

\[
 Q_0=\pi^2+a\gamma^2,\qquad
 P_0=\pi^3+\tfrac32a\gamma^2\pi,\qquad
 J=-3a^2\gamma^3,
\]

which has exactly the primitive (3:2,k=3) signature of the selected
((18,12)) descendant.  Inflating (pi) to a monic approximate root (h)
of (pi)-degree six gives

\[
 J(P_0(\gamma,h),Q_0(\gamma,h))=-3a^2\gamma^3 h_\pi.
\]

The unwanted ramification factor (h_\pi) is unavoidable under pure
composition.  All possible existence is therefore concentrated in the
allowed lower approximate-root corrections.  Compute the top two (h)-adic
Jacobian blocks and prove that the inherited tree/radius supports cannot
cancel the nonconstant part of (h_\pi).  This is a precise initial-form
lemma with a proof-shaped generalization; it does not confuse the empty pure
composition slice with the full pair.

