### Card 2 — Kummer/Fourier blocks and ramification absorption for (T<=4)

**Mechanism and new cross-connection.**  For a monomial-Jacobian descendant
(J(P,Q)=c\gamma^k), put (r=k+1) and (X=\gamma^r/r).  Then

\[
dP\wedge dQ=c\,dX\wedge d\pi.
\]

This is a constant-Jacobian equation over the cyclic Kummer extension

\[
k[X,\pi]\subset k[\gamma,\pi],\qquad \gamma^r=rX,
\]

not an ordinary Keller pair over (k[X,\pi]).  Decompose the free module by
(mu_r)-character,

\[
P=\sum_{a=0}^{r-1}\gamma^aP_a(X,\pi),\qquad
Q=\sum_{b=0}^{r-1}\gamma^bQ_b(X,\pi).
\]

After multiplying by (rX) and reducing (gamma^{a+b}) with
(gamma^r=rX), every Jacobian coefficient belongs to a declared character
and Newton-weight block.  The invariant block supplies (c); all other
character blocks vanish.  The factor (r=k+1) in the p.207 radius rule is
exactly the valuation pullback under this Kummer map.  That connects descent
radii, row 1's monomial bracket, Galois characters, and Appendix-II support
in one algebraic mechanism; it is not another numerical coincidence.

Use the primitive pair in section 3 as a positive control.  For the live
((18,12;\gamma^3)) target, (r=4) and a degree-six approximate root
introduces the explicit (h_\pi) factor.  The character blocks identify the
minimal correction modes capable of cancelling it.

**Dependencies.**  First close `OPEN[DESCENT-STATE-S3]` for the two (D=108)
variants.  Preserve their full (V'_3) data.  Compile allowed monomials from
proved radii only.  Use the corrected Appendix-II saturation discipline.

**Bounded quantity and cheapest discriminator.**  The first run has two
