   289	### 3.2 The γ-degree — PROVED-HERE
   290	
   291	Let `u_s`, `v_s`, `d_s`, `b`, `e` be as above; write `q(γ) := (c_0−e) + Σ_{i≥1} c_iγ^{i u_s}`
   292	(`deg_γ q < v_s` since `i u_s < v_s`), so that `σ = q(γ) + π γ^{v_s}` is a **polynomial**
   293	in `k[γ,π]`, and `y = θ^{-1} = γ^{-u_s}`. Put `ḡ(y,z) = Σ_{a,b} c_{ab} y^a z^b`,
   294	`B := deg_z ḡ`, `P(γ,π) := ḡ(γ^{-u_s}, σ)`.
   295	
   296	**(P1) `deg_π P = B = deg_x g`.** `P = Σ_{a,b} c_{ab} γ^{−a u_s} σ^b`; the coefficient of
   297	`π^B` is `γ^{B v_s}·Σ_a c_{aB} γ^{−a u_s}`, a nonzero Laurent polynomial (distinct `a`
   298	give distinct γ-exponents), so `deg_π P = B`. And `z = y − bx − e` is linear with `x`
   299	appearing to degree 1, so `deg_z ḡ = deg_x g`. Combining with Prop 6.3(2):
   300	**`deg_x g = u_s n/d_s`.** ∎
   301	
   302	**(P2) The `z`-leading coefficient of `ḡ` is `y^{n v_s/d_s}`, and `u_s + v_s = d_s`.**
   303	`P` monic in `π` forces `γ^{B v_s}Σ_a c_{aB}γ^{−a u_s} = 1`, so exactly one `a`
   304	survives, `a = B v_s/u_s`, with `c_{aB} = 1`. With `B = u_s n/d_s` this is
   305	`a = n v_s/d_s`. Since `deg ḡ = deg g = n` (linear change of coordinates) and the
   306	monomial `y^a z^B` occurs, `a + B = n(v_s+u_s)/d_s ≤ n`, i.e. `u_s + v_s ≤ d_s`;
   307	Moh's p.207 identity `u_s = d_s − v_s` is the equality case, so `y^{n v_s/d_s} z^{u_s n/d_s}`
   308	is a **top-degree** monomial and the leading form of `g` is
   309	`c·y^{n v_s/d_s}·(z\text{-part})^{u_s n/d_s}` with `z = y − bx` at top order. ∎
   310	
   311	**Two independent source confirmations.**
   312	*(i)* Moh p.208 (`SOURCE-READ`), the descended `(16,12)` pair:
   313	`h(x,y) = y^3(y−x) + b_1y^3 + b_2y^2 + b_3y + b_4`, `ḡ = h^4 + α_1h^3+α_2h^2+α_3h+α_4`,
   314	`deg_y α_i ≤ 3`. So the leading form of `ḡ` is `y^{12}(y−x)^4`. Apply (P2) to the
   315	descended pair: `n' = 16`, `d_2' = gcd(16,12) = 4`, `V_2' = 3` (p.207 table), so
   316	`u' = d_2' − v' = 1` and the predicted leading form is
   317	`y^{16·3/4}(y−x)^{16·1/4} = y^{12}(y−x)^4`. **Exact match.** And (P1) predicts
   318	`deg_x ḡ = u'n'/d' = 4`, which is what `h` of `deg_x h = 1` raised to the 4th power
   319	gives. **Exact match.**
   320	*(ii)* Moh p.211 (`SOURCE-READ`), the descended `(15,10)` pair with `V_2 = 3`:
   321	`h = (y^2 − x^2 + a_1y + a_2x + a_3)y^3 + (a_4x+a_5)y^2 + (a_6x+a_7)y + a_8`, so
   322	`h_top = y^3(y^2−x^2)`, `deg_x h = 2`, and `g_top = h_top^3 = y^9(y−x)^3(y+x)^3`.
   323	(P2) predicts `y^{15·3/5}·(deg-6 part) = y^9·(…)^6` ✓, and (P1) predicts
   324	`deg_x g = 2·15/5 = 6 = 3·deg_x h` ✓. (Here the `z`-part splits into two linear
   325	factors — the `V_2 = 3` subdisc split, "three subdiscs which contains 2, 2, 6 of roots",
   326	p.210 — so (P2) must be read as a statement about the `z^B`-coefficient in a
   327	`z`-adapted frame, which is exactly how it was proved.)
